WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.967
The model registry.

2
00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:07.733
At two in the morning, two questions matter: what is running in prod, and can we roll back right now?

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:12.233
A model registry is a versioned store for trained models.

4
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:14.231
It adds stage transitions.

5
00:00:14.231 --> 00:00:18.800
A version moves through stages, with review built into each promotion.

6
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.390
And it adds lineage.

7
00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:26.000
Every version links back to the exact run that produced it: code, data, and parameters.

8
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.733
Here is a version's journey.

9
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:31.700
It starts with no stage, freshly registered.

10
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.067
Then staging, where it is reviewed.

11
00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.700
Then production, serving real traffic.

12
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.500
And finally archived, when it is retired.

13
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.467
How is this different from tracking?

14
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:47.767
Tracking records everything you tried, hundreds of runs, most of them disposable.

15
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:50.490
The registry holds only the graduates.

16
00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.700
Runs are the lab notebook, and the registry is the pharmacy shelf.

17
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.100
Here it is in code.

18
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.900
We register a tracked run's model, and it becomes version three.

19
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.900
Then we point the production alias at version three.

20
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.466
Serving code never uses file paths.

21
00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:12.033
It asks for the production alias, so promotion and rollback are just moving a pointer.

22
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:15.033
Friday's promotion goes wrong.

23
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:20.667
Over the weekend, version three starts misclassifying eclipsing binaries as planets.

24
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:25.343
On-call moves the alias back to version two and restarts the pods.

25
00:01:25.343 --> 00:01:27.133
Ninety seconds, no rebuild.

26
00:01:27.433 --> 00:01:30.983
Monday, the lineage link shows the noisy data version.

27
00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:34.467
Registry plus tracking turned an outage into a query.

28
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:36.514
Let's recap.

29
00:01:36.514 --> 00:01:39.833
A registry holds versions, stages, and lineage.

30
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:43.333
Serving loads by name and alias, never by path.

31
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:45.767
So rollback is just moving a pointer.

32
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:50.210
Tracking is the notebook, and the registry is the shelf.

33
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.133
Next up: CI/CD for models.
